; =====================================================================
; capabilities.derived.cddl
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim.  RFC 9053 §8 defines COSE capabilities in
; prose and in three worked examples, and ships no CDDL.  Table 22
; (§10.1) prints the per-key-type capability lists in a bracket
; notation of its own ("[kty(1), crv]") that is not CDDL and not CBOR
; diagnostic notation.  Every rule below is this library's rendering.
; Cite RFC 9053 §8, §8.1, §8.2, §10.1 Table 22.
;
; Locator: RFC 9053 §8-§8.3, §10.1 Table 22, §10.2.
; Extraction status: derived.
;
; What it does NOT constrain: see README.md.  In one line - §8 says the
; capability list for an algorithm or key type is fixed by its IANA
; registration, so the real constraint is a registry lookup, and no
; schema can perform one.
; =====================================================================

; --- §8 the general shape --------------------------------------------
; "The capability structure is an array of values; the values included
; in the structure are dependent on a specific algorithm or key type."
COSE_Capability = [* any]

; --- §8.1 algorithm capabilities --------------------------------------
; "For the current set of algorithms in the registry other than
; IV-GENERATION (those in this document as well as those in [RFC8230],
; [RFC8778], and [RFC9021]), the capabilities list is an array with one
; element, the key type (from the "COSE Key Types" Registry).  It is
; expected that future registered algorithms could have zero, one, or
; multiple elements."
;
; §10.2 adds that the "COSE Algorithms" registry's new Capabilities
; column "has been populated with "[kty]" for all current,
; nonprovisional registrations", and that for IV-GENERATION "The
; Capabilities column for this registration is to be empty."
Algorithm_Capability_current = [ kty: int ]       ; every RFC 9053 alg
Algorithm_Capability_IV_GENERATION = []           ; alg 34, §10.2
Algorithm_Capability_future = [* any]             ; "zero, one, or multiple"

; §8: "For algorithm capabilities, the first element should always be a
; key type value if applicable, but the items that are specific to a key
; (for example, a curve) should not be included in the algorithm
; capabilities."  Lowercase "should" - advice, not BCP 14.

; --- §8.2 / §10.1 Table 22 key type capabilities ----------------------
; §8: "For a key, the first element should be the key type value."
; The six arms below are Table 22 as printed, in its own order.
Key_Type_Capability =
      Key_Type_Capability_OKP
    / Key_Type_Capability_EC2
    / Key_Type_Capability_RSA
    / Key_Type_Capability_Symmetric
    / Key_Type_Capability_HSS_LMS
    / Key_Type_Capability_WalnutDSA

Key_Type_Capability_OKP       = [ 1, crv: int ]   ; Table 22 [kty(1), crv]
Key_Type_Capability_EC2       = [ 2, crv: int ]   ; Table 22 [kty(2), crv]
Key_Type_Capability_RSA       = [ 3 ]             ; Table 22 [kty(3)]
Key_Type_Capability_Symmetric = [ 4 ]             ; Table 22 [kty(4)]
Key_Type_Capability_HSS_LMS   = [ 5, hash_alg: int ]
                                                  ; Table 22 [kty(5), hash algorithm]
Key_Type_Capability_WalnutDSA = [ 6, N: uint, q: uint ]
                                                  ; Table 22 [kty(6), N value, q value]
; §8.2 states the HSS-LMS element as "Algorithm identifier for the
; underlying hash function from the "COSE Algorithms" registry" and the
; WalnutDSA elements as "The N value (group and matrix size) for the
; key, a uint" and "The q value (finite field order) for the key, a
; uint".  `uint` is the source's own word; `int` for crv and hash_alg
; is OURS, from the registries' integer labels.

; --- §8.3 the cross-product form --------------------------------------
; The third example is "ECDH-ES + A256KW with an X25519 curve:
; 0x8101820104  / [1 \ OKP \],[1 \ OKP \, 4 \ X25519 \] /", which §8.3
; describes as the case that "just concatenates the two capabilities
; together".  Those five bytes are TWO CBOR data items, not one array:
; 0x8101 is [1] and 0x820104 is [1, 4].  A decoder that reads one item
; consumes two bytes and leaves three.  (Decoding OURS, from RFC 8949
; §3.1; RFC 9053 prints the hex and the annotation only.)
;
; §8 describes the fourth example's shape as "Each array element
; includes three fields: the algorithm identifier, one or more
; algorithm capabilities, and one or more key type capabilities."  That
; three-field element is what the rule below renders.  It is NOT a
; registered structure: §8.3 calls it "one of many encodings that could
; be used for that purpose."
Capability_Advertisement = [* Capability_Advertisement_Entry]
Capability_Advertisement_Entry = [
    alg: int / tstr,
    algorithm_capabilities: COSE_Capability / [+ COSE_Capability],
    key_type_capabilities:  COSE_Capability / [+ COSE_Capability]
]

; --- §8 immutability, which no schema can express ---------------------
; "Once defined by registration with IANA, the list of capabilities for
; an algorithm or key type is immutable.  If it is later found that a
; new capability is needed for a key type or algorithm, it will require
; that a new code point be assigned to deal with that."
